          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %em exotic smooth structure
            is, roughly speaking, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+manifold"} topological manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which makes the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} non-diffeomorphic
            to the smooth manifold given by some evident ‘standard’ smooth structure on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Mostly the term is used for smooth structures on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            and on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-spheres"} n-spheres
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            is exhibited by the identity
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
            is that given by the atlas of the two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hemispheres"} hemispheres
            as given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stereographic+projection"} stereographic projection
          %p
            For special values of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            there may exist smooth structure not equivalent to these. They are the
            = succeed "." do
              %em exotic smooth structures
          %p
            A classification of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/piecewise-linear+manifold"} PL
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+manifold"} topological
            structures on manifolds in dimension 5 and higher, in terms of various groups from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            (many not known) was established by
            %a{:href => "#KirbSieb"} Kirby and Siebenmann (1977)
            using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction+theory"} obstruction theory
          %h2#existence_and_examples Existence and Examples
          %h3#no_exotic_smooth_structure_in_dimensions_
            No exotic smooth structure in dimensions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq 3
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Rad"} Rado (1925)
            proved that in dimension 2 there are no exotic differentiable structures (or the uniqueness of the standard structure). The classification of 1-dimensional manifolds and the uniqueness of the smooth structure can be found in the Appendix of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Milnor1965b"} Milnor (1965b)
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Moise"} Moise (1952)
            proved that in dimension 3 there are no exotic differentiable structures, or to put in another way, 3-dimensional differentiable manifolds which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
            inherits a unique differentiable structure, no matter which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            it is considered to be embedded in.
          %h3#no_exotic_euclidean_space_away_from_dimension_4 No exotic Euclidean space away from dimension 4
          %p
            There exists a unique smooth structure on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\neq 4
            (
            %a{:href => "#Stallings62"}> Stallings 1962
            ).
          %h3#ExoticEuclideal4Space Exotic Euclidean 4-space
          %p
            There exists uncountably many exotic smooth structures on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            of dimension 4 (
            %a{:href => "#Gompf"}> Gompf 1985
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FreedTay"} Freedman/Taylor 1986
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Taubes"} Taubes 1987
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exotic+R%5E4"} exotic R^4
          %p
            There is a unique maximal exotic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            into which all other ‘versions’ of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            smoothly embed as open subsets (Freedman/Taylor 1986,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DeMFreed"} DeMichelis/Freedman 1992
          %p
            There are two classes of exotic
            = succeed "\u2018s:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            cannot be embedded in the 4-sphere
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^4
            (Gompf 1985, Taubes 1987) whereas a small exotic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            admits such an embedding (DeMichelis/Freedman 1992):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %em
                  large exotic
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
                is constructed by using the failure to smoothly split a smooth 4-manifold (the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K3+surface"} K3 surface
                for instance) as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+sum"} connected sum
                of some factors (where a topological splitting exits).
            %li
              %p
                The
                %em
                  small exotic
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
                (or
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em
                    ribbon
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mn 4
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-cobordism+theorem"} h-cobordism theorem
                in dimension 4 (
                %a{:href => "#Donaldson1"}> Donaldson 1987
                ,
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Donaldson2"} 1990
                %a{:href => "#BizGompf"} Bizaca and Gompf (1996)
                are able to present an infinite handle body of a small exotic
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
                which serve as a coordinate representation.
          %h3#exotic_
            Exotic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2 \times S^2
          %p
            There exists an infinite family of mutually non-diffeomorphic irreducible smooth structures on the topological 4-manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2 \times S^2
            (
            %a{:href => "#AkhmedovPark10"}> Akhmedov-Park 10
            ).
          %h3#Exotic4Spheres Exotic 4-spheres?
          %p
            It is open whether the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4-sphere"} 4-sphere
            admits an exotic smooth structure. See (
            %a{:href => "#FreedmanGompfMorrisonWalker09"} Freedman-Gompf-Morrison-Walker 09
            for review).
          %h3#Exotic7Sphere Exotic 7-sphere
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Milnor1956"} Milnor (1956)
            gave the first examples of exotic smooth structures on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7-sphere"} 7-sphere
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exotic+7-spheres"} exotic 7-spheres
            constructed in
            %a{:href => "#Milnor1956"} Milnor 1956
            are all examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibre+bundles"} fibre bundles
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4-sphere"} 4-sphere
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^4
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibre"} fibre
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-sphere"} 3-sphere
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+group"} structure group
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+group"} special orthogonal group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SO%284%29"} SO(4)
            (see also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/8-manifold"} 8-manifold
            the section
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "8-manifold#ExoticBoundary7Spheres"} With exotic boundary 7-spheres
          %p
            By the classification of bundles on spheres via the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clutching+construction"} clutching construction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+classes"} homotopy classes
            of maps
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                    %mo →
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 4
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3 \to SO(4)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 4
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_3(SO(4))
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "orthogonal+group#HomotopyGroups"} orthogonal group – Homotopy groups
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}\oplus\mathbb{Z}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,m)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mi m
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+m=1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morse+function"} Morse function
            with exactly two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+points"} critical points
            on the total space of the bundle, and hence this 7-manifold is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
            to a sphere.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractional+first+Pontryagin+class"} fractional first Pontryagin class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{p_1}{2} \in H^4(S^4) \simeq \mathbb{Z}
            of the bundle is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-m
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            theory and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hirzebruch%27s+signature+theorem"} Hirzebruch's signature theorem
            for 8-manifolds, a mod-7 diffeomorphism invariant of the manifold, so that it is standard 7-sphere precisely when
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mn 1
                        %mn 2
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi mod
                    %mn 7
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{p_1}{2}^2 -1 = 0 (mod 7)
          %p
            By using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+sum"} connected sum
            operation, the set of smooth, non-diffeomorphic structures on the
            = succeed "-sphere" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7-sphere"} 7-sphere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group"} cyclic group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 28
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z/{28}
            and Brieskorn (1966) found the generator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo #
                        %mi ⋯
                        %mo #
                        %mi Σ
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mn 28
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underbrace{\Sigma\#\cdots\#\Sigma}_28
            is the standard sphere.
          %p
            For review see for instance (
            %a{:href => "#Kreck10"}> Kreck 10, chapter 19
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#McEnroe15"} McEnroe 15
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exotic+7-sphere"} exotic 7-sphere
          %p
            From the point of view of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+8-manifolds"} M-theory on 8-manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/8-manifolds"} 8-manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with (exotic)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7-sphere"} 7-sphere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundaries"} boundaries
            correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/near+horizon+limits"} near horizon limits
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2+brane"} M2 brane
            spacetimes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{2,1} \times X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-branes"} M2-branes
            themselves would be sitting at the center of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7-spheres"} 7-spheres
            (if that were included in the spacetime, see also
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "M-theory+on+8-manifolds#MorrisonPlesser99"}> Morrison-Plesser 99, section 3.2
            )
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#Exotic8Sphere Exotic 8-sphere
          %p
            The abelian group of non-diffeomorphic structures with connected sum on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/8-sphere"} 8-sphere
            is the cyclic group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z/2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J-homomorphism"} J-homomorphism
            and is the first instance of an exotic sphere that does not bound a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallelizable+manifold"} parallelizable manifold
            (
            %a{:href => "#Amabel17"}> Amabel 17, Sec. 11
            ). It admits a metric of positive
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ricci+curvature"} Ricci curvature
          %h3#ExoticNSpheresForNGreaterThanFour
            Exotic
            = succeed "-spheres" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 5
          %p
            Via the celebrated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h+cobordism+theorem"} h cobordism theorem
            of Smale (
            %a{:href => "#Smale"}> Smale 1962
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Milnor1965a"} Milnor 1965
            = succeed "-sphere" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
            (for
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 5
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopy"} isotopy
            classes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Diff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0 (Diff(S^{n-1}))
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equator"} equator
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n-1}
          %p
            Then
            %a{:href => "#KervMil"} Kervaire and Milnor (1963)
            proved that for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 5
            there are only finitely many exotic smooth structures on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"} n-sphere
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
            (possibly none).
          %p
            By using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+sum"} connected sum
            operation, the set of smooth, non-diffeomorphic structures on the
            = succeed "-sphere" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
          %p
            The only odd-dimensional spheres with
            %em no
            exotic smooth structure are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-sphere"} 3-sphere
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^5
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 61
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{61}
            (
            %a{:href => "#WangXu16"}> Wang-Xu 16, corollary 1.13
            )
          %p
            In the range
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 5
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≤
                  %mn 61
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5 \leq n  \leq 61
            the only
            = succeed "-spheres" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em no
            exotic smooth structures are
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^5
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^6
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 12
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{12}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 56
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{56}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 61
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{61}
            (
            %a{:href => "#WangXu16"}> Wang-Xu 16, corollary 1.15
            ).
          %p
            It is conjectured that this exhausts in fact all examples of
            = succeed "-spheres" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 5
            (
            %a{:href => "#WangXu16"}> Wang-Xu 16, conjecture 1.17
            ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+topology"} differential topology
          %h2#references References
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exotic_sphere"} Exotic sphere
          %h3#for_the_mathematical_theory For the mathematical theory
          %p
            The first construction of exotic smooth structures was on the 7-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            in
          %ul
            %li#Milnor1956
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Milnor"}> John Milnor
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On manifolds homeomorphic to the 7-sphere
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/exotic.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9789812836878_0001"} doi:10.1142/9789812836878_0001
          %p (…)
          %ul
            %li#Smale
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Smale"}> Stephen Smale
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the structure of manifolds
            %li#Milnor1965a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Milnor"} John Milnor
                (1965),
                %em Lectures on the h-cobordism theorem
                (Princeton Univ. Press, Princeton)
            %li#KervMil
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michel+Kervaire"}> Michel Kervaire
                , ;
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Milnor"} John Milnor
            %li#KirbSieb
              %p
                Kirby, R.; Siebenmann, L. (1977)
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Foundational essays on topological manifolds, smoothings, and triangulations
            %li#Stallings62
              %p
                John R. Stallings,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The piecewise-linear structure of Euclidean space
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/papers/stallings2.pdf"} pdf
            %li#FreedTay
              %p Freedman, Michael H.; Taylor, Laurence (1986) “A universal smoothing of four-space”, J. Diff. Geom. 24, pp. 69-78
            %li#DeMFreed
              %p
                De Michelis, Stefano; Freedman, Michael H. (1992) “Uncountably many exotic
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            %li#Donaldson1
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Donaldson"} Simon Donaldson
                (1987) “Irrationality and the h-cobordism conjecture”, J. Diff. Geom. 26, pp. 141-168.
            %li#Donaldson2
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Donaldson"}> Simon Donaldson
                , (1990) “Polynomial invariants for smooth four manifolds”, Topology 29, pp. 257-315.
            %li#Gompf
              %p
                Gompf, Robert (1985) “An infinite set of exotic
                = succeed "\u2018s\u201D," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            %li#Taubes
              %p Taubes, Clifford H. (1987) “Gauge theory on asymptotically periodic 4-manifolds”, J. Diff. Geom. 25, pp. 363-430
            %li#BizGompf
              %p
                Bizaca, Z.; Gompf, Robert (1996) “Elliptic surfaces and some simple exotic
                = succeed "\u2018s\u201D," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            %li#Rad
              %p Rado, T. (1925) “Über den Begriff der Riemannschen Fläche” , Acta Litt. Scient. Univ. Szegd 2, pp. 101-121
            %li#Milnor1965b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Milnor"}> John Milnor
                , (1965b)
                %em Topology from the Differentiable Viewpoint
                (University Press of Virginia)
            %li#WangXu16
              %p
                Guozhen Wang, Zhouli Xu,
                %em The triviality of the 61-stem in the stable homotopy groups of spheres
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org\n/abs/1601.02184"}> arXiv:1601.02184
                )
            %li
              %p
                Llohann D. Sperança,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Pulling back the Gromoll-Meyer construction and models of exotic spheres
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.6039"} arXiv:1010.6039
            %li
              %p
                Llohann D. Sperança,
                %em Explicit Constructions over the Exotic 8-sphere
                (
                %a{:href => "https://www.ime.unicamp.br/~rigas/sigma8EncontroTopol.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SperancaExoticSpheres.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Amabel17
              %p
                ,
                %em Exotic spheres
                (
                %a{:href => "https://math.mit.edu/~araminta/Exotic.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#DRL10
              %p
                C. Duran, A. Rigas, Llohann D. Sperança,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Bootstrapping Ad-equivariant maps, diffeomorphisms and involutions
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ime.unicamp.br/~rigas/bootstrapping"} pdf
          %p
            On the open issue of exotic
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4-spheres"} 4-spheres
          %ul
            %li#FreedmanGompfMorrisonWalker09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Freedman"}> Michael Freedman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Gompf"} Robert Gompf
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+Morrison"} Scott Morrison
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Walker"} Kevin Walker
              = succeed "," do
                %em Man and machine thinking about the smooth 4-dimensional Poincaré conjecture
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0906.5177"} arXiv:0906.5177
          %p
            On exotic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2 \times S^2
          %ul
            %li#AkhmedovPark10
              Anar Akhmedov, B. Doug Park,
              %em
                Exotic smooth structures on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2 \times S^2
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.3346"}> arXiv:1005.3346
              )
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li#Kreck10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthias+Kreck"}> Matthias Kreck
                , chapter 19 “Exotic 7-spheres” of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Differential Algebraic Topology – From Stratifolds to Exotic Spheres
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rustam+Sadykov"}> Rustam Sadykov
                , Sections 7,8 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Elements of Surgery Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.ksu.edu/~sadykov/Lecture%20Notes/Surgery%20Theory.pdf"} pdf
            %li#McEnroe15
              %p
                Rachel McEnroe,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Milnor’ construction of exotic 7-spheres
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://math.uchicago.edu/~may/REU2015/REUPapers/McEnroe.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia (
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exotic_sphere#References"}> spheres
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exotic_R4#References"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi R
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^4
          %h3#ReferencesForApplicationsToPhysics For applications to physics
          %p
            The relevance of exotic smooth structure to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is tantalizing but remains by and large unclear. Some of the following references probably ought to be handled with care.
          %h4#general_relativity General relativity
          %p
            The argument that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exotic+spheres"} exotic spheres
            are to be regarded as
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+instantons"} gravitational instantons
          %ul
            %li#Witten85
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                , p. 12 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Global gravitational anomalies
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1103943444"} EUCLID
            %li
              %p
                Randy A. Baadhio,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the global gravitational instanton and soliton that are homotopy spheres
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1063/1.529078"} doi:10.1063/1.529078
          %p
            Further discussion of exotic
            = succeed "-manifolds" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            point of view is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carl+Brans"}> Carl Brans
                , Duane Randall,
                %em Exotic differentiable structures and general relativity
                Gen. Rel. Grav., 25 (1993) 205–220
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carl+Brans"}> Carl Brans
                ,
                %em Exotic smoothness and physics
                J. Math. Phys. 35, (1994), 5494–5506.
          %p
            The following paper contained a first proof to localize exotic smoothness in an exotic
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carl+Brans"}> Carl Brans
              ,
              %em Localized exotic smoothness
              Class. Quant. Grav., 11, (1994), 1785–1792.
          %p A more philosophical discussion can be found in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carl+Brans"}> Carl Brans
              ,
              %em Absolute spacetime: the twentieth century ether
              Gen. Rel. Grav. 31, (1999), 597–609
          %h4#generation_of_source_terms_fields Generation of source terms (fields)
          %p
            Brans conjectured in the papers above, that exotic smoothness should be a source of an additional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravitational field
            (Brans conjecture). This conjecture was confirmed for compact
            = succeed "-manifolds" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4
          %p
            Using the invariant of L. Taylor
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://de.arxiv.org/abs/math/9807143"} arXiv
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            in:
          %ul
            %li
              Jan Sładkowski
              %em Gravity on exotic R4 with few symmetries
              Int.J. Mod. Phys. D, 10, (2001) 311–313
          %h4#quantum_field_theory Quantum (field) theory
          %p
            The first real connection between exotic smoothness and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is Witten’s TQFT:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              %em Topological quantum field theory
              Comm. Math. Phys., 117, (1988), 353–386.
          %p and the whole work of Seiberg and Witten leading to the celebrated invariants.
          %p The relation to particle physics by using the algebra of smooth functions can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Jan Sładkowski,
                %em Exotic smoothness, noncommutative geometry and particle physics
                Int. J. Theor. Phys., 35, (1996), 2075–2083
            %li
              %p
                Jan Sładkowski,
                %em Exotic smoothness and particle physics
                Acta Phys. Polon., B 27, (1996), 1649–1652
            %li
              %p
                Jan Sładkowski,
                %em Exotic smoothness, fundamental interactions and noncommutative geometry
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9610093"} arXiv
          %p The relation between TQFT and differential-topological invariants of smooth manifolds was clarified in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hendryk+Pfeiffer"}> Hendryk Pfeiffer
                \:
                %em Quantum general relativity and the classification of smooth manifolds
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0404088"} arXiv
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hendryk+Pfeiffer"}> Hendryk Pfeiffer
                \:
                %em Diffeomorphisms from finite triangulations and absence of ‘local’ degrees of freedom
                Phys.Lett. B, 591, (2004), 197-201
          %p
            On the impact of exotic smooth structures on the spectrum of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operators"} Dirac operators
          %ul
            %li
              Ulrich Chiapi-Ngamako, M. B. Paranjape:
              %em Physics on manifolds with exotic differential structures
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13328"}> arXiv:2501.13328
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Ulrich Chiapi-Ngamako, M. B. Paranjape:
              %em Physics on manifolds with exotic differential structures
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13328"}> arXiv:2501.13328
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h4#ReferencesInStringTheory String theory
          %p
            An argument for interpreting exotic smooth spheres as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+instantons"} gravitational instantons
            and to cancel the gravitational
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomalies"} anomalies
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#Witten85"}> Witten 85
            ).
          %p
            The influence of exotic smoothness for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein models
            was discussed here:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthias+Kreck"}> Matthias Kreck
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Stolz"} Stefan Stolz
              %em
                A diffeomorphism classification of
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 7
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 7
                = succeed "-symmetry" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔰𝔲
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ×
                        %mi 𝔰𝔲
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ×
                        %mi 𝔲
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{su}(3) \times \mathfrak{su}(2) \times \mathfrak{u}(1)
              Ann. Math. 127, (1988), 373–388.
          %p A discussion of topological effects (also of string theory) in relation to exotic smoothness is in
          %ul
            %li
              Ryan Rohm,
              %em Topological Defects and Differential Structures
              Annals Of Physics, 189, (1989), 223–239.
          %h4#cosmology Cosmology
          %p An overview can be also found in
          %ul
            %li
              Jan Sładkowski,
              %em Exotic smoothness and astrophysics
              Act. Phys. Polon. B, 40, (2009), 3157–3163
          %h4#quantum_gravity Quantum gravity
          %p
            A first calculation of the state sum in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            by inclusion of exotic smoothness
          %ul
            %li
              Kristin Schleich, Donald Witt,
              %em Exotic Spaces in Quantum Gravity I: Euclidean Quantum Gravity in Seven Dimensions
              Class.Quant.Grav., 16, (1999), 2447–2469
          %p A semi-classical approach to the functional integral is discussed here:
          %ul
            %li
              Christofer Duston,
              %em Exotic smoothness in 4 dimensions and semiclassical Euclidean quantum gravity
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0911.4068"} arxiv
          %p The inclusion of singularities for asymptotically flat spacetimes is discussed here (with an example of a singularity coming from exotic smoothness):
          %ul
            %li
              Kristin Schleich, Donald Witt,
              = succeed "," do
